nodecode-recall is a rewrite of memeshee's memeshee/hermes-recall:

  https://github.com/memeshee/hermes-recall
  commit 99c9a6a4a290af705d42c7336cd7fb57ca3eb9d6
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 memeshee (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the whole behaviour of the plugin, in Common Lisp. The five tools
(recall_index, recall_search, recall_open, recall_stats, recall_forget), the
/recall command, the read policy (Nodecode's own home, virtual filesystems
and the credential-store denylist, judged on the resolved path so a symlink
cannot launder a secret), the default of ~/Documents alone with ~/Downloads
and ~/Desktop opt-in, the incremental walk with its file, time and size
budgets, the chunker, the synthetic name chunk that keeps a scanned PDF
findable, BM25 ranking with the original's deterministic boosts (a query word
in the path, in the file name, a phrase in order, a gentle recency favour),
the bracketed snippet, the ambient hint before the model answers, the
background refresh once an index exists, and the JSON shapes the tools
answer with.

Left out, and why:

  - SQLite FTS5. The rewrite keeps its index in one JSON file under the
    organism's home and rebuilds the term postings in memory, so a cell loads
    with no database binding and the file stays readable. The ranking is BM25
    computed here rather than by FTS5, and the stemmed/prefix behaviour of
    the unicode61 tokenizer is only the prefix half.
  - The @recall: context reference and its autocomplete. Nodecode has no
    context-reference seam; the same index is one tool call away, and the
    ambient hint covers the case it existed for.
  - The registered skill file. Its guidance is the (help :recall) topic, which
    rides every request as one line and is read in full on demand.
  - Python's zlib and zipfile extraction. A PDF is read through pdftotext and
    an Office file through unzip when the operator has them; the standard
    library gave the original both, which a cell does not get for free.
    Without them the file is still indexed by the words in its name.
